Subject: [PATCH 10179/11362] Record the measured suite decomposition and a
 NameError in a sealed guard

Three full-suite runs were needed because the first two were void. With
tmp_path_retention_count = 1, every pytest invocation deletes all but the most
recent /tmp/pytest-of-<user>/pytest-N, and the conductor runs pytest nearly
every iteration -- so it deleted the long run's tmp base out from under it,
mid-run. Run 3 used a private --basetemp and is the first honest measurement:
1,726 failed, 55,973 passed, 143 errors. The contamination SUPPRESSED the
count by turning failures into setup errors, so the earlier, lower numbers were
not better news.

The decomposition matters more than the total. Cross-test pollution dominates:
671 failures sit in two suite-only classes whose tests pass in isolation
(test_experiment_834 fails 12 in the suite, passes 69 alone). Fixing those
tests individually would be fixing the wrong thing.

Also recorded: a hypothesis of mine that was measured and rejected. I traced
ONE file to a KeyError in the fabrication gate's self-refresh helper and
reported it as the systemic cause. It is 98 of 1,726 -- 5.7%, 13 files -- and
the 13 modules carrying the by-path loading pattern produced zero failures.
Written down because the wrong version was briefly acted on.

Separately: scripts/test_suite_mutation_check.py:1197 calls _is_under(), which
is defined nowhere in that file. 18 failures, all reproducing in isolation.
It is a sealed guard and the pre-commit interlock for every commit here; the
--gate path still returns OK so the live path is unaffected, but the branch
would crash rather than cry wolf, and whatever it was written to detect is
currently undetectable. Landed 2026-08-25 in a conductor checkpoint commit.

